From 358c223ce3d60242a63c74514105d2edfdda4145 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 21:32:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep a numeric let/const in a raw double, item 3-3's second
 half

A function-body-top-level `let` or `const` the compiler can prove only ever
holds a number now lives in a CLR double, exactly as an eligible `var` already
did. Measured with `--local-alloc`, both arms from one tree: `let-binding` and
`const-binding` go 31.98 -> 0.00 B/iteration and 1 -> 3 numeric locals, which
is identical to `top-level-var`, the eligible floor, with all twelve other rows
byte-identical. The 1 -> 3 is the multiplier the measurement predicted: the
accumulator in `s = s + v * 2` is only provably numeric while `v` is, so
re-qualifying one binding re-qualifies the locals downstream of it.

A lexical binding carries two things a `var` does not, and both live in the
JSVariable cell this removes. The temporal dead zone is discharged rather than
deleted: the analysis already rejects any name referenced before its
declaration, so the throw is unreachable on exactly the names that qualify.
Const-ness needed one addition -- a write to a const is a TypeError raised by
the cell, so a const written anywhere is rejected outright rather than
specialized into a store that would silently succeed.

Only the NUMERIC tier admits lexical names; the JSValue tier stays closed to
them, because nothing there proves either obligation. A first attempt at this
item was withdrawn on a miscompile of nested-block bindings, and its recorded
reproduction is pinned here as a test: it is green against this change, and
also under BROILER_JS_REWRITER_INDEX_THRESHOLD and BROILER_JS_DEFER_IL=0, which
restore the front end as it was when the attempt was made. That defect is
therefore avoided rather than explained, which is why the reproduction stays.

VisitVariableDeclaration tests NumericStorage before the lexical branch rather
than after it: a numeric local's Expression is a boxing read, so assigning
through it is an assignment to a method call, which the IL backend rejects.

Adds LexicalNumericLocalTests (58 cases) and test262-lexical-declarations.txt,
a manifest for the let/const/block-scope paths no pinned manifest covered:
397 executed, 397 passed, 0 failed against this change.
